{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.96,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "polarity",
      "components": ["D1", "R14", "T8", "T1"],
      "pins": ["D1.1", "D1.2", "R14.1", "R14.2", "T8.1", "T8.2", "T1.3"],
      "nets": ["/NET0058", "/+24V_F", "GND"],
      "observed": "D1 is a 12 V zener with its cathode on /NET0058, the T8 gate node, and its anode on GND. R14 pulls that gate node toward /+24V_F, which is also T8's source. With T1 off, D1 clamps the gate near 12 V while the source is near 24 V, leaving T8 with about -12 V gate-to-source bias.",
      "expected": "For a P-channel MOSFET that should turn off when T1 is inactive, its gate should rise to the source voltage. Any gate-protection zener should clamp gate-to-source voltage, not clamp the gate to ground in a way that leaves the MOSFET turned on.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"/NET0058\") (node (ref \"D1\") (pin \"1\") (pinfunction \"K\")) (node (ref \"R14\") (pin \"1\")) (node (ref \"T8\") (pin \"1\") (pinfunction \"G\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"/+24V_F\") (node (ref \"R14\") (pin \"2\")) (node (ref \"T8\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"S\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(net (name \"GND\") (node (ref \"D1\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"A\")))"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(comp (ref \"D1\") (value \"12V\")"
        },
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(comp (ref \"T8\") (value \"AON6407\")"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "T8 remains biased on even when T1 is off, so the gate control cannot reliably disable the +24 V feed through T8; the downstream rail may remain powered when it is supposed to be switched off.",
      "confidence": 0.96
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The zener D1 clamps the P-channel T8 gate to ground rather than to its source. This leaves T8 with a strongly negative gate-to-source voltage in the nominal off state, defeating the gate pull-up and likely preventing the +24 V feed from being switched off."
}

